{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And this is the fashion of the menorah, etc.</b> The reason it was necessary to state this verse once more here, and it was not sufficient what was already said in parashat Terumah and in parashat Vayakhel, is resolved both according to the one who says the lamps were not fixed — for it comes to remove the obstacle of error drawn from the statement of ״בְּהַעֲלֹתְךָ״ (when you kindle), about which we said that he would disassemble them and then set them up again; and from this a rationale would emerge to say that the rest of the menorah too is not obligated to be made of beaten work (mikshah), for after all the lamps are included as part of the menorah, and we find that they are disassembled — and one would reason from them by the principle of 'a matter that was in the general rule and went out,' etc., to teach about the general rule — that although the verse said ״מִקְשָׁה״ (beaten work), this was only at first, but afterwards one may make it as he wishes. Therefore it says <b>and this is the fashion of the menorah, beaten work</b>, etc. — that so it stands, and from an inference you learn that the lamps went out of the general rule of the beaten work of the menorah, so you do not infer from them. And according to the one who says the lamps were fixed, it likewise comes to inform us that the menorah stood as a beaten work even after they had used it."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:52.747330919Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}